# Hypnosis Studio — environment
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. `.gitignore` covers .env and .env.* ; this example
# file is the one exception, so keep real secrets out of it.
#
# server.js reads .env from its own directory at startup (process.loadEnvFile),
# so `node server.js`, `npm start` and systemd all pick it up — no flag needed.
# Values already present in the real environment WIN over this file, so systemd
# beats a stale .env left in a deploy directory.
#
# The frontend is different: VITE_* variables are read by Vite at BUILD time and
# inlined into the bundle. Changing one means rebuilding, not restarting.
#
# PRODUCTION DOES NOT USE THIS FILE. The systemd unit loads
# /srv/hypnosis-studio/engine/api.env as an EnvironmentFile (DEPLOYMENT.md §83),
# so on the server the key belongs there, not here. Both work: the real
# environment wins over .env, so a systemd-supplied value beats anything left in
# a deploy directory. This file is for local development.
#
# Every value below is optional EXCEPT ELEVENLABS_API_KEY. The defaults shown
# are what the code actually falls back to — they are not suggestions.

# ─── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The only true secret. Without it the engine cannot voice anything, and a
# render fails at the TTS step rather than at startup.
# Billed per character, which is what MONTHLY_CHAR_BUDGET below guards.
#
# SCOPE: Text to Speech -> Access, AND User -> Read. The second one is new in
# #25: the server reads the remaining plan balance before it will let anyone
# pay, so a customer cannot buy a program the plan cannot voice. A key without
# it still works — the preflight logs the missing scope and falls back to the
# local monthly ledger rather than closing the shop.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# ElevenLabs' API root. Overridden only by the test suite, which points it at a
# fake provider on localhost. Leave unset in every real environment.
# ELEVENLABS_API_BASE=https://api.elevenlabs.io

# How long a credit-balance reading is reused before the provider is asked
# again. Keeps the preflight off the critical path of every page view.
# CREDIT_CACHE_MS=60000

# ─── Access ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Prototype gate on the render endpoint. Empty means no code is required, which
# is the current default — set it before anything is reachable publicly.
# ACCESS_CODE=

# ─── Payments (#22) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# DO NOT SET STRIPE_SECRET_KEY UNTIL #23 HAS SHIPPED.
#
# #22 added POST /api/checkout, which creates a Stripe Checkout Session. #23 is
# what makes a completed payment actually start a render, by verifying the
# webhook signature. Until that exists, setting the key here means the studio
# can take $39 and produce nothing. The endpoint answers 503 checkout_disabled
# while this is empty, which is the correct state today.
#
# Test keys (sk_test_...) are safe to use for local development at any time.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# The webhook signing secret (whsec_...) for the endpoint you register at
# /api/stripe/webhook. A DIFFERENT value from STRIPE_SECRET_KEY.
#
# This is the credential that authorises spending ElevenLabs credits: a
# signature-verified `checkout.session.completed` is what starts a render.
# Without it the endpoint answers 503 rather than trusting the request body,
# because an unconfigured webhook that accepted events would be an open
# "render this for free" endpoint.
#
# SETTING THIS CLOSES THE ACCESS_CODE PATH. POST /api/programs then answers
# 503 rendering_requires_payment. That is deliberate: ACCESS_CODE is a shared,
# unrate-limited string whose value is in a public git history (#32), and
# leaving it live beside a paid path means the paid path guards nothing.
#
# Locally, `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:4100/api/stripe/webhook`
# prints a whsec_ for the session.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Where Stripe sends the customer back after paying or cancelling. REQUIRED for
# checkout — the server refuses (503) rather than deriving it from the request's
# Host header, which a client can forge, and which would send the customer to
# someone else's site immediately after they paid us.
#
# No trailing slash needed; one is stripped if present.
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://hypnosisstudio.com

# The price, in the smallest currency unit. THIS is the price that is charged —
# the '$39' on the site is display text, and a test fails if the two disagree.
# Changing it means changing web/src/lib/data.ts too.
# PROGRAM_PRICE_CENTS=3900

# The site writes prices with a '$', so changing this without changing the site
# charges a different currency from the one the customer read. A test pins the
# default to usd for that reason.
# PROGRAM_CURRENCY=usd

# How many Checkout Sessions this service will ask Stripe for per minute,
# across all callers. /api/programs is gated by ACCESS_CODE and the quotas;
# /api/checkout cannot be — it is what a customer who has not bought anything
# reaches — so this is what bounds a loop against it. Rejected and unconfigured
# requests never spend a slot, because they never reach Stripe.
# CHECKOUT_MAX_PER_MINUTE=30

# How long a paid order whose render was REFUSED waits before it is refunded
# automatically (#26).
#
# A transient refusal (busy, a full disk) is answered 500 and Stripe retries,
# so the order may yet be fulfilled. A permanent one — the monthly allowance
# being spent — is answered 422, which Stripe never retries, so without this
# the order would sit paid and undelivered forever. Long enough for the first
# case, short enough that the second does not hold someone's money for a day.
#
# A retry arriving after the window finds an order already refunded, and a
# refunded order is never rendered — so the outcome is a refunded customer, not
# a program given away.
# REFUND_UNSTARTED_GRACE_MS=900000

# How many "send my link again" requests are served per minute, across all
# callers (#70). Lower than the checkout cap because this endpoint emails
# whoever is named in the request — without a cap it is a mail cannon pointed
# at any address someone types. Exceeding it changes nothing a caller can see:
# a different answer under load would leak whether an address has an order,
# one request at a time.
# RESEND_MAX_PER_MINUTE=5

# Stripe's API root. Overridden only by the test suite, which points it at a
# fake Stripe on localhost so "no request left the box" is an assertion the
# tests can actually make. Leave unset in every real environment.
# STRIPE_API_BASE=https://api.stripe.com

# ─── Delivery email (#28) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# A 15-20 minute render means nobody sits on the page, so a finished program is
# emailed to the address Stripe collected at checkout. The email carries the
# /program/<id> link, not the audio — the masters are hundreds of megabytes.
#
# Sent over an HTTP API rather than SMTP, because SMTP would mean a dependency
# and this repo has none. Defaults to Resend; swapping provider is one fetch in
# sendEmail() plus these three values.
#
# With any of EMAIL_API_KEY, EMAIL_FROM or PUBLIC_BASE_URL unset, nothing is
# sent — the order records `delivery: skipped` and the customer still has their
# page. It is not an error, but it does mean nobody is told their program is
# ready.
# EMAIL_API_KEY=

# The From: address. Must be on a domain verified with the provider, or every
# send is rejected. No default: sending from something that will bounce is
# worse than not sending.
# EMAIL_FROM=studio@hypnosisstudio.com

# The provider's API root. Overridden only by the test suite, which points it
# at a fake provider on localhost.
# EMAIL_API_BASE=https://api.resend.com

# Where a customer is told to write when something is wrong. The SERVER's copy,
# used in the delivery email; the site has its own (VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL below),
# inlined at build time. A test pins the two defaults together.
# SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@hypnosisstudio.com

# ─── Support address (#18) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Shown on the refund policy and linked in the footer. Read at BUILD time by
# Vite, so changing it means rebuilding the frontend — it is baked into the
# bundle, not read at runtime.
#
# Falls back to frank.bria@pm.me when unset. Set this in prod rather than
# editing the source.
#
# It must be monitored by a person: /refunds tells customers a person reads it.
# VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@example.com

# ─── Server ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# PORT=4100
# RENDERS_DIR=./renders                      # where job directories are written
# SEGMENT_CACHE_DIR=<RENDERS_DIR>/segment-cache

# Python interpreter for the engine. The default assumes engine/venv exists;
# override when the venv lives elsewhere or in a container.
# ENGINE_PY=./engine/venv/bin/python

# ─── Limits and cost control ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Renders started per calendar day, across all users.
# MAX_JOBS_PER_DAY=6

# ElevenLabs characters per calendar month. The server refuses a render it
# cannot pay for rather than discovering the ceiling mid-job, and refunds the
# ledger when a job fails.
# MONTHLY_CHAR_BUDGET=500000

# A worker exceeding this is killed. 45 minutes; a render is 15-20.
# HARD_TIMEOUT_MS=2700000

# ─── Retention ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# How long a finished render stays downloadable, in days.
#
# The server REFUSES TO START if this is shorter than what the site promises
# (see RETENTION_PROMISED_DAYS below). The site states the number in prose on
# /terms and /privacy, baked in at build time, so it cannot see this override —
# and a customer who was told they had thirty days discovers the difference in
# week three, having already paid. A failed deploy is the cheaper failure (#103).
#
# Longer than the promise is fine: we under-promised.
# RETENTION_DAYS=30

# What the SITE currently promises, in days.
#
# Not a bypass flag. The invariant is "never delete sooner than you promised",
# so shortening retention means changing what the pages say AND declaring the
# new promise here. A flag named ALLOW_SHORT_RETENTION would let someone
# silence the check without changing anything a customer reads.
#
# Defaults to the value baked into web/src/lib/legal.ts, which a test pins.
# RETENTION_PROMISED_DAYS=30

# Log what the sweep would delete without deleting it. Useful on first deploy.
# RETENTION_DRY_RUN=1

# How often the retention/stale-job sweep runs. Lowered by tests; leave alone.
# SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=60000

# ─── Engine internals ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Working dtype for the mix. Defaults to float32; float64 roughly doubles peak
# memory and is only worth it when chasing a numerical difference.
# HYPNO_DTYPE=float32

# Skip loading this file entirely. Set by the test harnesses, which spawn
# server.js from the repo root and set every variable they depend on explicitly
# — a developer's .env sits in that same directory, so any variable a test
# relies on the DEFAULT for would otherwise take the developer's value instead.
# HYPNO_NO_DOTENV=1

# Skip the shippability gate. For development only — it is the check that stops
# a silent or truncated program reaching a customer.
# HYPNO_SKIP_QA=1
